## Spec-compliance report — issue #59

**(a) Missing/partial requirements**

- "The four homepage cards render in the intended responsive grid (not squished/narrow) at both mobile and desktop widths" — **not visually confirmed**. Code-level reasoning supports it: `items-center` on a `flex-col` container shrinks its child to fit-content on the cross (horizontal) axis, which is exactly the root cause the issue describes; `items-stretch` removes that constraint so `Cards`' `grid @container` sees full available width. But this is inference from the class change, not observation.
- "No regression to the hero section's centering/spacing" — the modified `<div>` only wraps `Cards`; `HomeHero` is a sibling above it and its own markup/classes are untouched, so there's no code-level path for this change to affect hero centering. Not visually confirmed either.
- "Verified visually via playwright-mcp, not just by reading class names" — **not satisfied in this review session**. I started `next dev -p 3417` in the sandbox (confirmed "Ready", confirmed via `/dev/tcp` that both `127.0.0.1:3417` and `172.17.0.3:3417` accept connections from inside the container), then attempted `browser_navigate` to `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, the container's own IP, the docker gateway (`172.17.0.1`), `host.docker.internal`, and its resolved IP (`192.168.5.2`). Every attempt failed (`ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED` on loopback-style addresses, timeout on all `172.17.x` addresses). Per `.sandcastle/mcp-playwright-connect.sh`, playwright-mcp connects over CDP to a real Chrome running on the host that `run-sandcastle.ts` launches for the run — that Chrome has no route into this container's docker network for an ad-hoc `next dev` I started manually (no port was published to the host). This is an environment/infra limitation of this review session, not something more retries would fix, and not evidence about whether the diff itself works. I cannot personally attest to the acceptance criterion's required visual verification.

**(b) Scope creep**: None. The diff is a single class swap (`items-center` → `items-stretch`) on one wrapper `div`, matching the issue's own suggested fix verbatim ("e.g. drop `items-center` in favor of `items-stretch`"). Nothing else touched.

**(c) Implemented-but-wrong**: Nothing suspicious in the diff itself — it's a textbook, minimal application of the exact fix the issue proposed, with no adjacent classes disturbed (`justify-center`, `mt-24`, `flex-col` all preserved).

Net: the change is correctly scoped and plausible on inspection, but the acceptance criteria's explicit visual-verification bullet is unmet by this review due to sandbox network isolation between the CDP browser and the container running the dev server.
