## Spec

Diff (`items-center` → `items-stretch` on the `Cards` wrapper) is an exact, minimal application of the fix the issue itself proposed ("e.g. drop `items-center` in favor of `items-stretch`"). No scope creep — nothing else in the file was touched. Reasoning checks out: `HomeHero` is a sibling, not a flex child of the modified wrapper, so hero centering is unaffected; `flex-col`'s main-axis `justify-center` (vertical stacking) is preserved untouched, only the cross-axis (`items-*`) alignment changed, which is exactly what controls the child's shrink-to-fit vs. stretch sizing that the issue's root-cause analysis describes.

One acceptance-criteria bullet — "Verified visually via playwright-mcp, not just by reading class names" — could not be independently re-confirmed in this review sandbox: both my Spec sub-agent and I hit the same infra constraint (playwright-mcp's browser runs on the host and has no network route into this container's dev server; the sub-agent exhaustively tried localhost/container-IP/gateway/host.docker.internal address variants, all failed). I attempted to reproduce the implementer's own documented workaround (inlining the rendered HTML+compiled CSS into a `data:` URL for the host browser to load) but the resulting payload (~220KB base64) was prohibitively expensive to route through this review context and I stopped short of completing it. This is judged to be a sandbox-environment limitation rather than a code defect: the CSS mechanism here is deterministic, well-understood behavior (flexbox `align-items` cross-axis sizing), the implementer's commit message documents genuine visual verification via the same technique at both 1200px and 375px, and nothing in the diff itself is suspect.

**Summary**: Standards axis — 0 findings, clean. Spec axis — 0 defects; one acceptance-criterion bullet (independent visual re-verification) unmet in this review session for infra reasons, not a code issue. Overall: no maintainer-blocking findings.

